  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "Analysis of device-based mobility data during Hurricanes Helene and Milton reveals that local activity resumed in roughly 4.5 days across all areas. However, external reconnection varied significantly, taking 5 days in coastal-urban Florida compared to 11 days in inland-rural Appalachia.",
      "why": "Disaster recovery efforts typically focus on immediate local restoration, but these findings demonstrate that rural inequality stems specifically from prolonged external isolation, requiring planners to reorient assistance toward inter-regional links."
    }
